WEBVTT

00:00.770 --> 00:04.610
Write the equation of the
function f of x graphed below.

00:04.610 --> 00:06.614
And so we have this
clearly periodic function.

00:06.614 --> 00:08.280
So immediately you
might say, well, this

00:08.280 --> 00:12.050
is either going to be a sine
function or a cosine function.

00:12.050 --> 00:14.240
But its midline
and its amplitude

00:14.240 --> 00:17.300
are not just the plain vanilla
sine or cosine function.

00:17.300 --> 00:18.800
And we can see that
right over here.

00:18.800 --> 00:21.250
The midline is halfway
between the maximum point

00:21.250 --> 00:22.420
and the minimum point.

00:22.420 --> 00:26.070
The maximum point right over
here, it hits a value of y

00:26.070 --> 00:27.100
equals 1.

00:27.100 --> 00:29.070
At the minimum points,
it's a value of y

00:29.070 --> 00:30.170
is equal to negative 5.

00:30.170 --> 00:32.800
So halfway between
those, the average of 1

00:32.800 --> 00:36.010
and negative 5, 1 plus
negative 5 is negative 4.

00:36.010 --> 00:38.370
Divided by 2 is negative 2.

00:38.370 --> 00:42.500
So this right over
here is the midline.

00:42.500 --> 00:44.330
So this is y is
equal to negative 2.

00:47.630 --> 00:50.895
So it's clearly shifted down.

00:53.160 --> 00:54.910
Actually, I'll talk
in a second about what

00:54.910 --> 00:56.830
type of an expression
it might be.

00:56.830 --> 00:59.400
But now, also, let's
think about its amplitude.

00:59.400 --> 01:00.970
Its amplitude--
that's how far it

01:00.970 --> 01:03.580
might get away from the
midline-- we see here.

01:03.580 --> 01:06.120
It went 3 above the midline.

01:06.120 --> 01:08.470
Going from negative 2 to 1,
it went 3 above the midline

01:08.470 --> 01:09.680
at the maximum point.

01:09.680 --> 01:13.430
And it can also go 3 below the
midline at the minimum point.

01:13.430 --> 01:16.195
So this thing clearly
has an amplitude of 3.

01:23.416 --> 01:25.040
So immediately, we
can say, well, look.

01:25.040 --> 01:28.240
This is going to have
a form something like f

01:28.240 --> 01:35.260
of x is equal to
the amplitude 3.

01:35.260 --> 01:37.110
We haven't figured
out yet whether this

01:37.110 --> 01:39.310
is going to be a cosine
function or a sine function.

01:39.310 --> 01:41.060
So I'll write "cosine" first.

01:41.060 --> 01:45.107
Cosine maybe some coefficient
times x plus the midline.

01:45.107 --> 01:46.690
The midline-- we
already figured out--

01:46.690 --> 01:49.880
was minus 2 or negative 2.

01:49.880 --> 01:51.940
So it could take
that form or it could

01:51.940 --> 01:58.800
take f of x is equal to 3
times-- it could be sine

01:58.800 --> 02:02.440
of x or sine of some
coefficient times x.

02:02.440 --> 02:07.320
Sine of kx minus 2 plus
the midline-- so minus 2.

02:07.320 --> 02:09.274
So how do we figure
out which of these are?

02:09.274 --> 02:11.690
Well, let's just think about
the behavior of this function

02:11.690 --> 02:13.880
when x is equal to 0.

02:13.880 --> 02:16.610
When x is equal to
0, if this is kx,

02:16.610 --> 02:20.580
then the input into the
cosine is going to be 0.

02:20.580 --> 02:22.311
Cosine of 0 is 1.

02:22.311 --> 02:24.810
Whether you're talking about
degrees or radians, cosine of 0

02:24.810 --> 02:25.570
is 1.

02:25.570 --> 02:28.690
While sine of 0-- so
if x is 0, k times

02:28.690 --> 02:31.240
0 is going to be
0-- sine of 0 is 0.

02:31.240 --> 02:34.010
So what's this thing doing
when x is equal to 0?

02:34.010 --> 02:37.860
Well, when x is equal to
0, we are at the midline.

02:37.860 --> 02:40.480
If we're at the midline, that
means that all of this stuff

02:40.480 --> 02:42.890
right over here evaluated to 0.

02:42.890 --> 02:46.100
So since, when x equals 0, all
of this stuff evaluated to 0,

02:46.100 --> 02:47.830
we can rule out the
cosine function.

02:47.830 --> 02:50.770
When x equals 0 here, this
stuff doesn't evaluate to 0.

02:50.770 --> 02:53.280
So we can rule out this
one right over there.

02:53.280 --> 02:55.070
And so we are left with this.

02:55.070 --> 02:59.030
And we just really
need to figure out--

02:59.030 --> 03:02.250
what could this
constant actually be?

03:02.250 --> 03:03.710
And to think about
that, let's look

03:03.710 --> 03:06.620
at the period of this function.

03:10.890 --> 03:11.390
Let's see.

03:11.390 --> 03:13.480
If we went from
this point-- where

03:13.480 --> 03:19.680
we intersect the midline--
and we have a positive slope,

03:19.680 --> 03:22.620
the next point that we do
that is right over here.

03:22.620 --> 03:24.195
So our period is 8.

03:29.530 --> 03:31.650
So what coefficient
could we have

03:31.650 --> 03:35.710
here to make the period of
this thing be equal to 8?

03:35.710 --> 03:37.880
Well, let us just
remind ourselves

03:37.880 --> 03:40.260
what the period of sine of x is.

03:40.260 --> 03:42.510
So the period of
sine of x-- so I'll

03:42.510 --> 03:46.660
write "period" right
over here-- is 2pi.

03:46.660 --> 03:50.030
You increase your angle by
2 pi radians or decrease it.

03:50.030 --> 03:52.720
you're back at the same
point on the unit circle.

03:52.720 --> 03:56.550
So what would be the
period of sine of kx?

03:56.550 --> 04:02.170
Well, now, your x, your input
is increasing k times faster.

04:02.170 --> 04:04.890
So you're going to get to the
same point k times faster.

04:04.890 --> 04:10.300
So your period is going
to be 1/k'th as long.

04:10.300 --> 04:15.030
So now your period is
going to be 2 pi over k.

04:15.030 --> 04:20.500
Notice, as x increases, your
argument into the sine function

04:20.500 --> 04:22.100
is increasing k times as fast.

04:22.100 --> 04:23.630
You're multiplying it by k.

04:23.630 --> 04:25.130
So your period is
going to be short.

04:25.130 --> 04:29.490
It's going to take you less
distance for the whole argument

04:29.490 --> 04:33.200
to get to the same point
on the unit circle.

04:33.200 --> 04:35.920
So let's think
about it this way--

04:35.920 --> 04:45.660
so if we wanted to say 2
pi over k is equal to 8,

04:45.660 --> 04:48.030
well, what is our k?

04:48.030 --> 04:50.530
Well, we could take the
reciprocal of both sides.

04:50.530 --> 04:56.520
We get k over 2 pi
is equal to 1/8.

04:56.520 --> 04:59.560
Multiply both sides by 2pi.

04:59.560 --> 05:05.360
And we get k is
equal to-- let's see.

05:05.360 --> 05:05.920
This is 1.

05:05.920 --> 05:06.962
This is 4.

05:06.962 --> 05:09.800
k is equal to pi/4.

05:09.800 --> 05:10.610
And we are done.

05:10.610 --> 05:13.370
And you can verify that by
trying out some of these points

05:13.370 --> 05:14.340
right over here.

05:14.340 --> 05:21.200
This function is equal to 3
sine of pi over 4x minus 2.
